  [case id=sup-031-identical-config-distinct-identities level=concurrency packs=sched
   [tags supervise identity uniqueness 765 895]
   [title [#two supervisors started with IDENTICAL config in one process get distinct identities#]]
   [meta [#
ruling: SPF-1 (#895)
   #]]
   [doc [#
Supervisor identity comes from the EVALUATOR's own monotonic handle
counter (supervise.md §9), read as the `__cx_close_id__` stamped on a
[?subscribe] handle — core surface, present in every §4 profile, no
capability. Nothing about the identity may derive from the CONFIG:
same policy, same child list, same process, still two supervisors,
so still two identities (and so two disjoint sets of channels).

This case is the uniqueness proof for the id source. It also guards
the regression that motivated it: the previous source armed a
240-hour [$sched:after] and kept the timer's synthesized name, which
made a supervisor's identity the property of an OPTIONAL pack.

The ids themselves are engine-internal and deliberately NOT asserted
(their absolute values depend on how many handles the program has
stamped); the CONTRACT is that they differ.
   #]]
   [in-cx [# [empty] #]]
   [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/supervise' :as sup]
[?let [= $c [?channel name="sup-uniq-park" buffer=1]]
[= $p [policy strategy=:one-for-one max-restarts=3]]
[= $kids ([child name="a" [fn [?fn () [?receive from=$c]]]])]
[= $s1 [$sup:start $p $kids]]
[= $s2 [$sup:start $p $kids]]
[= $_1 [$sup:stop $s1]]
[= $_2 [$sup:stop $s2]]
[identity distinct=[not [= $s1@sup $s2@sup]]
          workers-distinct=[not [= $s1@worker $s2@worker]]]]
#]]
   [out-text [#
[identity distinct=true workers-distinct=true]
#]]
  ]
]
